> Task :populateArtifactURLs
I checked the file — it already matches the "Current URL values" shown in the task: `source-code-url`, `repository-url`, `test-code-url`, `documentation-url`, and `description` are all present and non-blank. The only field that's actually absent is `language`.

Since spring-boot-opentelemetry is a Java auto-configuration module (core Spring Boot OpenTelemetry SDK integration, part of the `module/spring-boot-opentelemetry` subproject introduced in Spring Boot 4.x's modularized layout — the same layout used by the sibling `spring-boot-micrometer-tracing-opentelemetry:4.1.1` entry merged in commit `5a109f4b16`), it is **not language-specific**: it's plain Java, consumable from Kotlin/Groovy/Scala equally, with no Kotlin extensions or language-specific API surface. So the `language` field correctly stays absent rather than being filled.

Per the instructions — "Fill only missing fields" and "Do not modify fields that already contain a non-blank value" — there is nothing to write here. All populated fields stay as-is, and the one missing field (`language`) is intentionally left absent because the library doesn't qualify. No edit to `index.json` is needed.

`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
- Spring Boot 4.x restructured the monorepo into a `module/` directory per auto-configuration concern (e.g., `module/spring-boot-opentelemetry`, `module/spring-boot-micrometer-tracing-opentelemetry`) — this is why the existing `test-code-url` template already points at `module/spring-boot-opentelemetry/src/test` instead of the older flat `spring-boot-actuator-autoconfigure`-style layout used pre-4.0.
- The `language` field in this schema is reserved for libraries whose entire API is only usable from one JVM language (e.g., a Kotlin coroutines wrapper, a Groovy DSL). A Java auto-configuration class is callable from any JVM language, so it's never "language-specific" even when the consuming app happens to be Kotlin or Groovy.
- The "fill only missing fields" + "don't touch non-blank values" guardrail exists precisely to make idempotent re-runs of this kind of maintenance task safe — rerunning it against an already-complete entry is a no-op rather than silently overwriting a previously-verified URL with a possibly-worse guess.
